  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "Reading the tree from creation",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Reading the tree from creation. In every Fano tree satisfying condition N (every atom keeps a line, and its surviving lines through the points it lost at receiving cuts form distinct proper subsets of its lines; true whenever every atom has at most four cuts), the atoms are exactly the twin classes of creation (two lines that create at each other and at the same other lines); for each atom X, the other atoms whose lines create at the same proper subset of X's lines are exactly those beyond one cut where X receives, so every cut is read once, from its receiver, with its far side; and these splits give the tree, each cut running from the far side's atom next to the receiver into the receiver. So native creation recovers the directed handle tree of every such Fano tree. Proved by hand from ticket A and the block lemma; checked on every Fano tree of at most five atoms, on random trees of up to thirty atoms wherever N holds, and on the exact creation map of ticket B's six-atom counterexample to the source rule; where N fails the rule loses the tree. Computed on BRACE-TREE C's 111 fresh spaces with exact creation maps: in every Fano tree each atom is exactly one twin class; through plane glue the rule does not extend as stated, since a glue line can join an atom's class (155 atoms) and an atom can split into three twin pairs (10 atoms), though no twin class ever mixes two atoms.",
